recordtest: the widening of the original's 32-bit file records, tested on the PC game's data.

Run:  python3 tools/recordtest/run.py [--iso DIR]      (needs clang and the game's files; about 40 s)

The default data directory is ../pc-xl/iso next to the repository. The test regenerates nothing: it fails
if game/src/port_records.c is out of date with tools/genlayouts.py.

What it checks (see the comments in run.py and docs/RECORDS.md):
  run.py        reads the 32-bit and native layouts from clang's record-layout dumps and, from them alone,
                computes the native bytes every file record must become; compares them with what the game's
                port_widen.c writes (recordtest.c), for all PRMs (standalone and in every WAD), every WAD's
                track sections, vertices and faces, CONFIG.DAT, SAVEGAME.BIN, MENU.DAT, random records of every
                table type, random PRM images and damaged ones (under ASan and UBSan);
                narrow(widen(x)) == x (padding bytes aside); every widened PRM is walked with the real native
                structs the way LoadPrmCommon does.
  loadtest.c    the game's own loaders (scene.c, track.c, wadfile.c, init.c, radscn.c, ships.c, weapon.c, ...)
                run on every track and on InitResident, with a recording allocator: what they build is compared
                with the files, and the heap use is compared with the original's (a model of its allocator
                with 32-bit sizes). build_loadtest.py builds it.
